   134	so `v(f)=min` of the two. For the equivalence, `v(f)>=-(d+1)` gives
   135	`deg_A P<=d+1` and `deg_A Q<=d`, and `f_{-(d+1)} = C_{d+1}(V^2)+V D_d(V^2)`
   136	is odd exactly when `C_{d+1}=0`. For (2.4), `v(UQ)>=0` forces `deg_A Q<=-1`,
   137	i.e. `Q=0`. QED
   138	
   139	Two remarks that matter later. First, `S \ Phi = Spec RR[A^-1]` is
   140	`G_m x A^1`: the interior divisor `Phi=V(A,U)` is exactly `T=infinity`, and the
   141	whole `A`-adic tower of the horn lives at the *opposite* end `T=0`. Second, the
   142	constraint `deg_A <= 2` is a bound on the order of the pole of `f` along the
   143	prime divisor `{T=0}` of a compactification of `S \ Phi`, plus one parity
   144	condition on the leading coefficient. Under the source chart `iota` of
   145	`theta:(3.12)` one has `T=x^-2`, `V=x^-1+xy`, `{x,y}=1`; so `v` is (twice) the
   146	monomial divisorial valuation of `C(x,y)` with `v(x)=-1`, `v(y)=+1`, whose
   147	residue field is `C(xy-bar)`. The `A`-filtration of the horn is therefore a
   148	classical filtration at one divisorial place at infinity of the plane. No flag,
   149	place or series is identified here: `{T=0}`, `Phi`, the target place
   150	`q_infty` of `theta:§4`, and the generic ramified factor of `theta:(1.7)`
   151	remain four distinct objects, and none of them is used below.
   152	
   153	## 3. Why the naive leading-term induction breaks at A-degree 2
   154	
   155	The review located a free compensator in `[A^1]O` (`review:§6`). The true
   156	situation is stronger and settles the question of bottom-up induction for good.
   157	
   158	Write `P=sum C_i A^i`, `Q=sum D_i A^i`, `R_0=sum E_i A^i`, `S_0=sum L_i A^i`
   159	with `C_i,D_i,E_i,L_i in C[Z]`, and abbreviate the level-0 residue data
   160	`p=C_0, q=D_0, r=E_0, s=L_0`. Reading off (1.1) term by term in `A` (each
   161	summand of `E` and of `O` is one of `A^2, H=A+A^2Z, c=2+4AZ` times a
   162	bilinear expression, so this is a finite collection of index shifts, not an
   163	expansion) gives the two support laws:
   164	
   165	```text
   166	[A^n]O involves C,E at level <= n+1 and D,L at level <= n;
   167	[A^n]E involves all four at level <= n.
   168	```
   169	
   170	and, isolating in each the part where one index is `n+1` resp. `n`:
   171	
   172	```text
   173	[A^n]O = 4(n+1)( C_{n+1} r' - p' E_{n+1} ) + M_n,                    (3.1)
   174	[A^n]E = (4n+2)( D_n r' - p' L_n )
   175	         + 2( 2n C_n s' - C_n' s ) + 2( q E_n' - 2n q' E_n ) + Lam_n,  (3.2)
   176	```
   177	
   178	where `M_n` depends only on levels `<= n` and `Lam_n` only on levels `<= n-1`.
   179	
   180	Both displayed formulas were checked against the promoted, independently
   181	reviewed `A`-linear computation. For `n=0`, (3.1) with
   182	`M_0=-2(q's-qs')` reproduces `theta:(3.4)` verbatim at the minimal jet, and for
   183	`n=1`, (3.2) reproduces the review's pre-substitution collection
   184	`-3KC+(3KZ/2)C'-KE'+6D(3Z^2-1)-12ZL+2KZ` (`review:§3`) exactly, with `+2KZ` as
   185	the level-0 remainder. This is the only place the frozen `A`-linear data is
   186	used, and it is used as a control, not as an input.
   187	
   188	**Lemma 3.1 (Bezout surjectivity).** If `gcd(p',r')=1` in `C[Z]` then
   189	`(X,Y) -> X r' - p' Y` is surjective onto `C[Z]` with kernel exactly
   190	`{(p'T, r'T) : T in C[Z]}`.
   191	
   192	Immersivity of the residue parametrization -- promoted at `theta:§1.2, §2.1` --
   193	is precisely `gcd(p',r')=1`.
   194	
   195	**Theorem 3.2 (formal unobstructedness along Phi).** Let `(p,q,r,s)` satisfy
   196	`gcd(p',r')=1` and `2(qr'-p's)=kappa != 0`. Then the constraint system
   197	`E=kappa, O=0` has a solution in the completion
   198	
   199	```text
   200	RRhat = C[Z][[A]] + U C[Z][[A]]
   201	```
   202	
   203	with that residue datum. The solution set is nonempty at every level and has a
   204	free `C[Z]` at each level; no level is ever obstructed.
   205	
   206	*Proof.* The bracket formulas (1.1) are `A`-adically continuous, so
   207	`E=kappa, O=0` in `RRhat` is exactly the sequence of coefficient equations
   208	`[A^0]E=kappa`, `[A^n]E=0` (`n>=1`), `[A^n]O=0` (`n>=0`). `[A^0]E=kappa` is the
   209	residue equation, satisfied by hypothesis. Recur in the order
   210	
   211	```text
   212	level 0  ->  (C_1,E_1)  ->  (D_1,L_1)  ->  (C_2,E_2)  ->  (D_2,L_2)  -> ...
   213	```
   214	
   215	At the `(C_{n+1},E_{n+1})` step, `M_n` in (3.1) is already determined, and by
   216	Lemma 3.1 the equation `4(n+1)(C_{n+1}r'-p'E_{n+1}) = -M_n` is solvable, with a
   217	free `T in C[Z]`. At the `(D_n,L_n)` step, everything in (3.2) except
   218	`(4n+2)(D_n r'-p'L_n)` is already determined -- `C_n,E_n` were fixed at the
   219	previous step -- and Lemma 3.1 solves it, again with a free `T`. QED
   220	
   221	**Corollary 3.3.** No bottom-up induction on the `A`-adic hierarchy can ever
   222	produce an obstruction, at any residue point of the seven-parameter family, at
   223	any `T_0` stratum, and at any level. The mechanism of `theta:(3.10)` -- an
   224	uncancellable leading term in `Z` on one side of `[A^1]O` -- is destroyed at
   225	`A`-degree 2 for a reason that recurs at every higher level: the level-`(n+1)`
   226	even data enters `[A^n]O` *only* through the Bezout combination
   227	`C_{n+1}r'-p'E_{n+1}`, which is a surjection. Symmetrically the level-`n` odd
   228	data enters `[A^n]E` only through `D_n r'-p'L_n`, also a surjection. The
   229	compatible finite layer `theta:(2.9)` is therefore not a coincidence but the
   230	first two steps of Theorem 3.2.
   231	
   232	This is the exact answer to "which cancellation becomes possible": *both*
   233	Bezout compensators, not just the one the review displayed, and at every level.
   234	It also relocates the whole problem. A formal solution along `Phi` always
   235	exists; the entire content of any `A`-degree obstruction is **polynomiality**,
   236	i.e. termination of the recursion. Obstructions must therefore be read off the
   237	*top* of the tower, never the bottom.
   238	
